METHOD
The verifier works in the exact real cyclotomic field
  Q(zeta), zeta=2*cos(pi/40).
It reconstructs the degree-16 minimal polynomial of zeta from Phi_80,
isolates zeta as the unique root in (199/100,2) by an exact rational
Sturm sequence, and decides every nonzero field-element sign by exact
rational interval refinement.

For all 190 unordered pairs of the 20 even-sector modes, it constructs
exactly:
  mu_(i,j)=(lambda_i*lambda_j)^2,
  the scaled exterior-square residue for rows {0,2} and columns {0,17}.
It groups equal bases in the cyclotomic field, proves the strict order
of all 131 distinct bases by exact Sturm signs, and forms every upper
tail after multiplication by mu^5.  The displayed tail is strictly
negative.

As an independent normalization and orientation replay, the verifier
constructs the 20-by-20 integer fusion matrix N_2^(19), computes its
tenth power, and checks the exact identity
  sum_groups scaled_residue*mu^5
    = 16*(19+1)^2
      * det((N_2^(19))^10)_[{0,2},{0,17}]
    = 6400*25181925.
Thus the negative upper tail coexists with a strictly positive complete
anchored determinant.

The verifier also evaluates, in the same exact field, every algebraic
power-convex call
  sum_(mu>=c) scaled_residue*mu^4*(mu-c)
for the interior counterexample parameters and for the numerical
near-zero wall case (K,Q,S)=(19,9,19).  Every call is nonnegative.
The latter computation proves that the small negative floating-point
values at the simple-current target are grouping/cancellation artifacts,
not counterexamples to the algebraic power-convex criterion.

The same exact procedure at (K,Q,S,r)=(20,1,18,10) disproves the
uniform power-convex criterion.  Here r=floor(K/(2Q)) is the last
ordinary/stable power before the affine wall.  The verifier groups 210
mode pairs into 173 exact bases, proves their order in
Q(2*cos(pi/42)), checks the complete spectral sum against the positive
integer determinant 13227930891, and finds four strictly negative
power-convex calls.  Thus the two positive cases above validate the
implementation but do not extend to a uniform call theorem.

The isolating interval contains zeta itself because
  2*cos(pi/40) > 2-pi^2/1600 > 199/100
from cos(x)>1-x^2/2 and pi^2<10, while zeta<2.

